From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ophuscado <postbox@ophuscado.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 08:08:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] soc: apple: pmp: Number the AdvertiseAcks from 0

The firmware registers its tunables and IOReport channels one Advertise
at a time, and the driver answers each with an index and a size. The
driver numbered them from 1 (the list's length after the push) and gave
an index to a property it could not find, while set_ioreg looks the
index up in a list that starts at 0, so the last entry is out of range.

macOS, traced booting on an M2 Ultra under m1n1's hypervisor (794
Advertises), numbers from 0 and answers a property it lacks with INDEX 0,
SIZE 0 without taking an index. Do the same.
---
 drivers/soc/apple/pmp.rs | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/pmp.rs b/drivers/soc/apple/pmp.rs
index 9a46852a9..f11043160 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/pmp.rs
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/pmp.rs
@@ -330,8 +330,13 @@ fn register_ioreg(&self, addr: u64) -> Result<u64> {
                 dev_info!(self.dev, "unknown property {:?}", name_str);
             }
         }
-        state.ioreg_entries.push(size, GFP_KERNEL)?;
+        // As macOS answers: indices from 0, and a property the tree lacks gets INDEX 0, SIZE 0
+        // without taking an index.
+        if size == 0 {
+            return Ok((OPC_REGISTER_IOREG | OPC_ACK_MASK) << OPC_SHIFT);
+        }
         let index = state.ioreg_entries.len() as u64;
+        state.ioreg_entries.push(size, GFP_KERNEL)?;
         let msg = (OPC_REGISTER_IOREG | OPC_ACK_MASK) << OPC_SHIFT | (index << 32) | size as u64;
         Ok(msg)
     }
